= Session 2: Drafting and Revising

#table(
  columns: (auto, auto, 1fr),
  align: (left, left, left),
  [*Time*], [*Segment*], [*Activity*],
  [0–5 min], [Warm-up],
  [Display three anonymous claims from the exit tickets. As a class, decide which is strongest and why, and revise one weak claim together.],
  [5–12 min], [Mini-lesson],
  [Teach how to address a counterclaim using the frame: "Some people argue that \_\_\_. However, \_\_\_." Show transition words for adding, contrasting, and concluding (_furthermore_, _on the other hand_, _in conclusion_). Model a hook and a closing call to action.],
  [12–37 min], [Independent drafting],
  [Students draft their essays using their planners. Structure: introduction with hook and claim, two or three body paragraphs (reason plus evidence), one paragraph with counterclaim and rebuttal, and a conclusion. While students write, hold brief conferences with students flagged from the exit tickets.],
  [37–45 min], [Peer review],
  [Partners exchange drafts and use the checklist to mark: Is the claim clear? Does each reason have evidence? Is a counterclaim addressed? Are transitions used? Each reviewer writes two compliments and one suggestion.],
  [45–50 min], [Reflection and wrap-up],
  [Students revise one sentence based on feedback, then complete the reflection: "The most persuasive part of my essay is \_\_\_. Next time I will improve \_\_\_." Collect drafts or assign completion for homework.],
)

== Peer Review Checklist

- #box(width: 0.8em, height: 0.8em, stroke: 0.5pt) The claim is clear and arguable.
- #box(width: 0.8em, height: 0.8em, stroke: 0.5pt) Each reason is supported by specific evidence.
- #box(width: 0.8em, height: 0.8em, stroke: 0.5pt) A counterclaim is acknowledged and answered.
- #box(width: 0.8em, height: 0.8em, stroke: 0.5pt) Transitions connect ideas between paragraphs.
- #box(width: 0.8em, height: 0.8em, stroke: 0.5pt) The conclusion restates the claim and gives the reader a call to action.

